# pnpm installs a flat, symlink-free node_modules here instead of its default
# content-addressed store layout.
#
# ## Why
#
# `apps/mobile`'s native Android build cannot complete against pnpm's default
# layout on Windows, for two compounding reasons:
#
#   1. **Path length.** pnpm nests every package under
#      `.pnpm/<name>@<version>_<hash>/node_modules/<name>`, where the hash is
#      long. CMake refuses to place an object file whose full path exceeds 250
#      characters (`CMAKE_OBJECT_PATH_MAX`), and `react-native-screens`'
#      object directory alone reached 222 before any filename was appended.
#      The repository path is only 45 characters — the budget goes to the
#      store layout, so moving the checkout does not buy enough back.
#
#   2. **Symlinks.** Each entry in a package's `node_modules` is a symlink
#      into that store. CMake records resolved real paths while ninja re-reads
#      the symlinked ones, so the generated manifest never converges and the
#      build dies with `ninja: error: manifest 'build.ninja' still dirty after
#      100 tries`.
#
# Hoisting removes both at once, and it is React Native's own documented
# guidance for pnpm. Windows long-path support does not substitute: CMake's
# 250-character limit is its own check, and long paths do nothing about the
# symlink mismatch.
#
# ## What this costs, and what to watch
#
# pnpm's default layout enforces a real discipline: a workspace can only
# import what it declares, because nothing else is reachable. Hoisting makes
# every transitive dependency importable from anywhere, so an undeclared
# import now resolves silently on this machine and in CI — and breaks only
# for whoever installs with a different linker, or when the transitive
# dependency is dropped by an unrelated upgrade.
#
# One thing does catch it, partially and by accident, and it is worth knowing
# about before someone "fixes" it: **no Dockerfile copies this file**, so the
# container images run their `pnpm install` under pnpm's default isolated
# layout. Inside an image, a package can still only see what it declares. The
# `images` job in .github/workflows/pr.yml builds those images on every pull
# request, so an undeclared import that this file lets through on a developer
# machine fails there instead — at review time rather than at deploy time.
#
# That is a backstop, not a guarantee: it only covers code the API and web
# images actually build and load, and it says nothing about apps/mobile. Treat
# a missing `package.json` entry as a real defect when reviewing an import,
# rather than trusting install to surface it.
node-linker=hoisted
